    answer: "The answer: In Maryland, a PE stamp typically costs $800–$2,500 for straightforward residential work — house plans, additions, deck and garage framing — and $2,500–$12,000 or more for commercial structural sets, depending on building size and complexity. These are typical market ranges, not firm quotes: the final price depends on scope, site conditions, and how complete your drawings already are. Most residential stamps turn around in 3–7 business days, while commercial sets take 1–3 weeks. Price and speed win here — a complete submittal with a clean Maryland PE stamp is the fastest path through plan check.\\n\\nA Maryland stamp is not a rubber stamp. The Maryland Board for Professional Engineers requires the sealing engineer to hold an active Maryland PE license and to have exercised responsible charge — real supervisory control over the engineering decisions, not a signature on someone else's drawings. The seal certifies the engineer designed or directly supervised the work, and the board disciplines engineers who seal work they did not control. When you pay for a stamp, you are paying for the engineering judgment and liability behind it, which is why legitimate firms will not seal drawings they have never reviewed.\\n\\nMaryland's cost drivers are geographic. The DC-metro premium is real: Montgomery County and Prince George's County run some of the most rigorous residential plan reviews in the state, and engineering fees in the I-270 corridor run higher than in western Maryland or the Eastern Shore. Baltimore City adds its own review layer with city-specific requirements. Sites near the Chesapeake Bay fall under Critical Area rules and floodplain requirements that add structural and civil scope. Expansive soils in parts of central Maryland and wind exposure along the Bay push foundation and lateral design beyond the minimum, and each of those adds engineering hours.\\n\\nPermitting follows the site, not the state. A house in Montgomery County goes through the Department of Permitting Services with county amendments; the same house in unincorporated Anne Arundel County faces a different checklist; Baltimore City runs its own building code enforcement. Your engineer confirms the authority having jurisdiction before pricing the job, because the review clock, the amendment package, and the inspection sequence all change the engineering scope. Get a priced scope in writing, ask for the turnaround in business days, and confirm the sealing engineer is verifiable on the Maryland board roster.",
